hey i'm praya i'm 26 years old my name  is erica i'm 37 years old  my name is matt edwards i was diagnosed  with covet in july  i contracted covert on march 23rd  seven months into my covert journey and  it is now october i still haven't  recovered from  any of the crippling fatigue nearly  seven months later and i'm still  unwell i am still a broken woman  [Music]  by now we all know the most common  symptoms of kovid 19  dry cough the high fever  the loss of taste or smell but  some people are beginning to feel the  long-term effects of coronavirus  some of these symptoms are having a  devastating impact  on people's lives the first two weeks  were relatively easy looking back  and it just got worse the more i  relapsed a lot of people talk about  having a fatigue that can't get like a  state of being at three o'clock in the  morning  all day long and that's kind of the way  that mine is  i could not even walk to the bathroom  alone the fatigue is so bad  i couldn't sit in the shower and wash my  hair i didn't have the energy to keep my  arms above my head  extreme fatigue is an often reported  symptom  but long covet seems to affect every  part of the body  with people reporting cognitive  difficulties anxiety depression  and headaches it can cause heart  problems such as irregular heartbeat  chest pain lung problems such as  shortness of breath  and gastrointestinal problems like  diarrhea  the way that i explain it to people is  it's kind of like getting up out of bed  in the morning  and then reaching your hand in a hat you  have to pull out a symptom  and you got like you know maybe 50  different symptoms that you can pull out  and then whatever you pull out of the  hat is  what you have for the day i was 26 when  i got it and i was so healthy i had no  underlying health conditions  no prior illnesses and here i am seven  months later and  i have heart irregular heartbeat heart  palpitations chest pain  really bad um brain issues muscle spasms  numbing all of this as a 26 year old and  i just think long covert needs to be  part of the discussion because at the  moment all it is is  older people are going to die  the nhs estimates around 60 000 people  in england could be suffering from long  covert symptoms more than three months  after catching the virus  it could be more it could be less and  because kovat19  is relatively new there is still a lot  we don't know about the long-term  offense we find that of all the people  that are symptomatic with covid  about one in 20 will actually have  symptoms still  at two months afterwards and one in 45  will have symptoms for longer than three  months  it does seem that having more than five  symptoms in the first week  seems to be a strong risk factor for  long covid it's slightly more likely if  you're  a female and this may be something to do  with the difference in immune response  to viruses in women and men  it is a mentally testing it's it's not  something i would wish on  even my worst enemy there have also been  reports of the impact of long covered on  mental health  causing anxiety and stress that's the  most untalked about thing  and also the most challenging thing they  just can't believe the amount of  messages i get each day from people  saying no one believes me  they think you know it's just mental  illness and they're just delusional  it's really awful and it's only because  it's not being spoken about enough  well for example my wife's a nurse and  uh  and you know she she's regularly trying  to be there for me with it but  you know she's regularly seeing patients  in the icu  every day with covid and then she sees  me you know  dealing with still symptoms way after  the fact and  it's even hard for her to understand at  times because she had coveted too  and it didn't affect her that way i had  three migraines yesterday  i was an absolute emotional mess i had  nothing under control i was just  crying the whole afternoon it makes you  question yourself a lot  you know are you just seeing things are  you just experiencing things like  i have a super understanding amazing  employer  for now i'm okay but if of course this  goes on for too much longer i don't know  what the situation is of course  my sick leave is not unlimited  now that more evidence of long covert is  coming to light there have been calls  for more support to be made available  health secretary matt hancock has even  been asked about  it in parliament people of all ages  affect are affected by long covert  and people who are who are younger who  might think that they're unlikely to get  severe symptoms or die  of covid can still have a terrible  illness  that completely changes their lives for  months months and months  the uk government responded by  announcing a 10 million pound  investment in october 2020 to fund long  covert clinics  across england it follows the setup of a  clinic in the italian city of genoa  also looking into sufferers of long  covet i think this is vitally needed  whether 10 million pounds is going to be  enough to deal with this  we've yet to see i think it may need  considerable more funding than that  but i think this is really great that  the government has put forward this  funding  without official guidance people have  been finding comfort in sharing their  experiences  on online forums one long covered  facebook page  has tens of thousands of members who  post stories of their relapses  symptoms and recovery being able to be  on a support group on facebook has  probably helped more than anything i  mean that's the only thing that sort of  kept me sane because  when you hear so many people have it and  when everyone's saying it's just a  two-week illness  and you'll get over it and you're not by  month three and then month four  and month five and you're still so  unwell it's so unnerving and the only  thing that keeps you sane  is talking to other people who have long  covered i was of course  thinking well maybe i have a terminal  illness maybe maybe i've got some  some form of cancer that i've been you  know  stricken with and  of course after several tests it was  none of those things  um and being able to be a part of a  support group and knowing that there are  so many other people who all have the  same neurological symptoms as me  uh as really helped  that's the only you know framework  around the pandemic is that  young people will probably be okay and  that older people might die  but that's not the truth there are  there's this whole in between which are  thousands of people are getting long  covered and there's no  answers seek whatever support you can in  my case  the support groups have been more help  than any doctors or any medical  professionals  so far so make sure that you you make  friends in the same situation
 

